<StatePage title="California 20-Day Preliminary Notice: Service & Timing" description="Check California preliminary notice recipients, the twenty-day lookback for late service, direct-contractor lender notice, and preparation and service records." canonical="https://mechanicslienform.com/preliminary-notice/california/" schema={schema}>
<article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 prose prose-lg"><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/mechanics-lien/">Document guides</a> › California preliminary notice</nav><h1>California twenty-day preliminary notice</h1><p>The preliminary notice identifies furnishing on a private construction project before a later payment claim. It is distinct from recording a mechanics lien. Start this workflow when project work begins so a collection dispute does not reveal an earlier missed task.</p>
<h2>Identify the recipients from the contract chain</h2><p>Section 8200 generally identifies the owner or reputed owner, direct contractor or reputed direct contractor, and construction lender or reputed lender, if any. Laborers are exempt. A claimant contracting directly with the owner still gives notice to the construction lender or reputed lender, if any. Determine the route from the actual relationship rather than the business's job title.</p><p>Draw the owner, direct contractor, your hiring party, and any intervening subcontractor on a project worksheet. Obtain ownership and financing information from reliable project and property records. Save the source and investigation date. Good-faith research should not be assumed to cure every recipient or address error.</p>
<h2>Calendar first furnishing and the late-notice lookback</h2><p>Section 8204 generally calls for notice within twenty days after first furnishing. Late notice protects work within the twenty days before service and afterward, rather than automatically restoring all earlier furnishing. Design services have a specific provision in subsection (b).</p><p>Keep delivery tickets, time sheets, and scope records that identify when furnishing began. If service is late, separate earlier charges from later work in the ledger for legal review. A late notice is not an excuse to invent a new first-furnishing date or treat ordinary corrective work as a new project.</p>
<h2>Prepare the statutory content without simplifying the warning</h2><p>Read sections 8102 and 8202 for general content and the required private-project notice statement. Gather claimant and recipient information, the property identification, work description, and estimated price information required for the notice. Review the complete statutory text before using a generic template.</p><p>Compare the property description with reliable records and the contract's project identifier. Check whether the form's estimate reasonably describes the furnishing contemplated. If a new contract, property, or materially changed scope creates uncertainty, obtain advice before assuming the original notice resolves it.</p>
<h2>Service and evidence are part of the task</h2><ol><li>Verify each required recipient and the legally appropriate address.</li><li>Choose a method allowed by the notice service provisions beginning at section 8100.</li><li>Keep an exact copy of the signed notice and any attachments.</li><li>Retain the mailing or delivery evidence and applicable service declaration.</li><li>Record the service date independently of the preparation date.</li></ol><p>An ordinary email or portal upload should not be assumed to complete statutory service. A file labeled “notice sent” is less useful than evidence showing the document, recipient, address, method, and date. Review returned mail promptly while corrective action remains possible.</p>
<h2>Continue to the later claim only if needed</h2><p>After notice, maintain invoices, approved changes, payment credits, and furnishing records. If payment remains outstanding, follow the <a href="/mechanics-lien/california/">California mechanics lien guide</a> and <a href="/mechanics-lien/form/">claim preparation checklist</a>. The later recording and owner-service steps have separate requirements.</p><p>A <a href="/notice-of-intent-to-lien/">collection notice of intent</a> does not replace the preliminary notice. When payment arrives, review a <a href="/lien-waiver/conditional/">conditional waiver</a>; if a claim is already recorded, also review its <a href="/mechanics-lien/release/">discharge</a>.</p><h2>Notice questions</h2>{faqs.map(f=><details class="rounded-lg border px-5"><summary class="cursor-pointer py-4 font-semibold">{f.question}</summary><p>{f.answer}</p></details>)}
<h2>Official California sources</h2><ul>{[8102,8200,8202,8204].map(n=><li><a href={'https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum='+n+'.'}>Civil Code section {n}</a></li>)}</ul><p>This private-project guide consolidates the former twenty-day-notice article. Public projects and bond claims need separate analysis. Use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/">state directory</a> for other property locations.</p></article></StatePage>